import{_ as n,o as t,c as a,ak as i}from"./chunks/framework.B8jO0odJ.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Chainy learning foundations","description":"The current GDD defines the learning experience.\\nThis page explains how its activities support learning and what their results can show.\\nIt is a documentation specification, n","frontmatter":{"title":"Chainy learning foundations","description":"The current GDD defines the learning experience.\\nThis page explains how its activities support learning and what their results can show.\\nIt is a documentation specification, n","source":"docs/learning-foundations.md","category":"Learning content","sourceUpdated":"2026-11-11T12:24:37+08:00","outline":[2,3]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"content/docs/learning-foundations.md","filePath":"content/docs/learning-foundations.md"}'),r={name:"content/docs/learning-foundations.md"};function o(s,e,c,d,l,h){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<div><h1 id="chainy-learning-foundations">Chainy learning foundations<a class="header-anchor" href="#chainy-learning-foundations" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h1><p>The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/game-design-lesson-collection.html">current GDD</a> defines the learning experience. This page explains how its activities support learning and what their results can show. It is a documentation specification, not a claim of delivered gameplay or proven learning gains. Development waits for final owner approval.</p><h2 id="current-teaching-design">Current teaching design<a class="header-anchor" href="#current-teaching-design" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Chainy introduces Web3, Blockchain, and AI to ages 12-17 through offline 3D exploration. The worlds unlock in that order. Each world has 13 lessons in prerequisite order, including three distinct minigames. Ordinary encounters introduce a concept and record its encyclopedia entry. Minigames ask the learner to apply a concept through routing, inspecting, ordering, testing, labeling, or correcting evidence. The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/planning/a-z-complete-lesson-quiz-source-draft.html">source pack</a> preserves the 26 book terms, 13 separately sourced AI terms, and source limitations.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Learner action</th><th>Feedback and observable evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Introduction and collection</td><td>Find the active lesson, inspect its object or guide, and read its explanation</td><td>The term is introduced and recorded; finding an object alone does not show understanding</td></tr><tr><td>Minigame practice</td><td>Complete the lesson&#39;s specific rule using the supplied evidence</td><td>Explain the error and permit retry; successful completion shows performance within that activity</td></tr><tr><td>Required world review</td><td>Answer 13 questions, one per lesson, and correct every wrong response</td><td>Keep first-response correctness separate from assisted correction; unlock the next world after completion</td></tr><tr><td>Revisit</td><td>Read recorded entries or replay supported practice</td><td>Revisiting supports recall; repeated correct answers are not automatically independent assessment</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The owner confirmed full 13-question coverage and correction until all questions are resolved. It is a completion rule, not a percentage-based mastery threshold. Keep interrupted or abandoned attempts distinct from completed reviews. The GDD specifies the exact review sequence, feedback, and saved states.</p><h2 id="one-small-content-sheet">One small content sheet<a class="header-anchor" href="#one-small-content-sheet" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Each lesson needs a stable ID, source locator, one objective, explanation, visible example, player action, success condition, feedback, and corresponding review question. Use the GDD&#39;s lesson panels and question bank as the working payload rather than maintaining competing copies here. The nine games have different rules; their learning value comes from what the player inspects and changes, not merely from varied themes.</p><p>All current AI activities are scripted offline teaching simulations. Explain actual dataset, training, model, prediction, and validation concepts without saying that the app fits a model from the learner&#39;s examples. Blockchain activities compare supplied records and illustrate conditions or disclosure; a local match does not establish network consensus or historical truth. There is no economy loop, live wallet, exchange, investment instruction, or real transaction.</p><h2 id="writing-and-review">Writing and review<a class="header-anchor" href="#writing-and-review" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Use short English instructions, familiar examples, and essential cues that work without audio. Do not equate chronological age with reading level, prior technology knowledge, or accessibility needs. Check that the lesson teaches what its question asks, that wrong alternatives are plausible, and that feedback explains why an answer works. Test a fresh example where practical instead of relying only on a memorized definition. Avoid timed reading, unexplained technical vocabulary, or progress shown only through color.</p><p>Sherwin owns design decisions; Sean owns authored documentation and source wording. Technical, UI, and art review retain their separate responsibilities. Subject or educator review is still needed where source accuracy or teaching suitability is uncertain. These review needs are content-readiness checks, not claims of school approval or assumed human availability.</p><h2 id="evidence-and-scope-boundary">Evidence and scope boundary<a class="header-anchor" href="#evidence-and-scope-boundary" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>Collection completion, first-response accuracy, correction completion, usability, and engagement measure different things. None alone establishes learning improvement. Repeated in-game practice is separate from any adviser-reviewed research pre/post instrument. The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/qa-evaluation.html">evaluation plan</a> owns evidence handling and reporting.</p><p>The original encyclopedia PDF is not present in this repository; current book explanations derive from its contents inventory. Question wording and answer keys remain draft until reviewed. Teacher services, export/transfer workflows, Wordle, BlockMatch, economy systems, and actual model training are outside this design, with no promised later delivery. The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/planning/lesson-collection-delivery.html">delivery preparation plan</a> preserves the approval boundary and November 21, 2026 pre-final requirements.</p><h2 id="historical-teaching-rationale">Historical teaching rationale<a class="header-anchor" href="#historical-teaching-rationale" aria-label="Link to this section">​</a></h2><p>The <a href="/Chainy-Docs/content/docs/archive/pre-lesson-collection-2026-11-11/learning-foundations.html">archived learning foundations</a> preserves the previous restoration, classifier, and vocabulary-game proposals. Those statements are historical and do not override the current GDD.</p></div>',1)])])}const g=n(r,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
